Scenario 1 (Continue Funding Large Farms), The method works effectively at first if Ontario keeps giving large farms priority because of their better return on public money. Efficiency improves and production rises throughout the first three to five years. The impression that investing in large farms is the right choice is reinforced by the dominance of the competitive intensification loop (R1). However, the strain on shared resources is growing as a result of this expansion. The commons depletion loop is accelerated by large farms' continued use of expensive inputs to scale output (R2). Water systems are under more stress, soil health starts to deteriorate, and environmental quality progressively deteriorates. The system depends more and more on high-input production by years five through ten in order to sustain output levels. Although overall output may stay high, it becomes more expensive and unstable. Yields are impacted by environmental deterioration, particularly when there is climatic stress. 
Scenario 2 (Fund Technology for Small Farms), In this scenario, Ontario reallocates funds to small and mid-sized farms, emphasizing technology (precision agriculture, soil monitoring, water management tools) that increases productivity and sustainability. The outcomes are sluggish at first. Small farms appear to have a poorer short-term return on investment and do not produce the same instant output gains as large farms. Nonetheless, the commons are less stressed by this action. Smaller farms often utilize a wider range of techniques and work at a lower intensity, and technology increases their productivity without using excessive amounts of resources. The commons depletion loop is slowed as a result (R2). As more small farms continue to operate, system diversity rises by years 4 7. More performers participate in the production, which lessens the requirement for excessive intensification. Long-term sustainability is enhanced when soil and water systems stabilize or deteriorate more slowly.
Scenario 3 (Hybrid Strategy), With this strategy, Ontario distributes funds equally between small farms (for sustainability and resilience) and large farms (for efficiency). Early on, the system gains from both increased involvement and high production.Large farms continue to drive the intensification cycle (R1), whereas small farms employing more sustainable methods lessen its effects. The commons depletion cycle is slowed but not eliminated as a result (R2). Environmental pressure is still there by years 4–7, although it is happening more slowly than it was before. The system's reliance on high-intensity production is lessened and variety is preserved thanks to small farms.The system finds a compromise by years 7–10: comparatively high productivity, less environmental stress, and increased resilience as compared to a large-farm-only approach.